UP-1 — renderer call-site updates (the mechanical part of the change)
=====================================================================

THE GENERAL ISSUE
-----------------
The confinement for the `local-file-download` / `local-file-save-from-arraybuffer`
IPC channels needs to know which folder a given download is allowed to land
in. That folder is known only at the call site. So making the channel safe is
a two-sided change: the privileged side validates, and every renderer caller
has to declare what its base is.

This file lists the renderer-side edits that accompany the core. They are
deliberately boring, and that is the point — a security patch whose call-site
changes all look obviously correct is a security patch whose call-site changes
were actually reviewed. There are exactly TEN call sites across the whole
repository; the full inventory is below so it can be checked for completeness.


PATTERN 1 — the exporters (8 of the 10 call sites)
--------------------------------------------------
Files in `newIDE/app/src/ExportAndShare/LocalExporters/`:

| File | line | `basePath` passed |
|---|---|---|
| `LocalElectronExport.js` | 157 | `context.exportState.outputDir` |
| `LocalHTML5Export.js` | 159 | `context.exportState.outputDir` |
| `LocalCordovaExport.js` | 157 | `context.exportState.outputDir` |
| `LocalOnlineElectronExport.js` | 153 | `temporaryOutputDir` |
| `LocalOnlineWebExport.js` | 144 | `temporaryOutputDir` |
| `LocalOnlineCordovaExport.js` | 157 | `temporaryOutputDir` |
| `LocalOnlineCordovaIosExport.js` | 156 | `temporaryOutputDir` |
| `LocalFacebookInstantGamesExport.js` | 180 | `temporaryOutputDir` |

Each already had the variable holding the folder it was exporting into; it
simply was not passed through. One added line each:

```diff
     await downloadUrlsToLocalFiles({
       urlContainers: [...],
+      basePath: context.exportState.outputDir,   // or temporaryOutputDir
       onProgress: ...,
       throwIfAnyError: ...,
     });
```

WHY THIS IS CORRECT: every `filePath` handed to this helper is a path INSIDE
the export output folder — the exporter is building a directory tree there and
each downloaded resource is placed somewhere under it. So declaring that
folder as the base admits every legitimate download and nothing else. There is
no per-call judgement here, which is the property that makes these eight safe
to review at a glance.

Note that `basePath` was ALREADY a required field of
`downloadUrlsToLocalFiles`' input type — it was already a compile-time
requirement, and the implementation just ignored it. No call site needed its
types loosened; if a reviewer finds one that did, that is a finding.


PATTERN 2 — the cloud-project resource download (the interesting one)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
File: `newIDE/app/src/ProjectsStorage/CloudStorageProvider/CloudProjectResourcesHandler.js:64-71`

```diff
       await downloadUrlsToLocalFiles({
         urlContainers: [ { url: resourceUrl, filePath: targetPath } ],
+        basePath: path.dirname(targetPath),
         onProgress: () => {},
         throwIfAnyError: false,
       });
```

This is the one call site worth reading twice, because it is the one where the
destination is chosen by something other than the code: `targetPath` comes
either from a save dialog (`askUserForDownloadDestination`) or from the OS
downloads folder. `path.dirname(targetPath)` is therefore correct and
deliberate — the folder the USER picked is the trust anchor for that one
download, and it is declared explicitly rather than defaulted.

It is also the call site with the most security relevance, because
`resourceUrl` is derived from the project: a shared or downloaded `.json`
project file names its resources, so both the URL and (indirectly) the
destination are attacker-controllable inputs. Before the fix, a project could
name `https://attacker.example/x.png` and the cloud session cookie would have
gone with it.


PATTERN 3 — the resource mover (`save-from-arraybuffer`, the only caller)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
File: `newIDE/app/src/ProjectsStorage/LocalFileStorageProvider/LocalFileResourceMover.js:70-88`

```diff
 const downloadBlobToLocalFile = async (
   blobUrl: string,
   filePath: string,
+  basePath: string
 ): Promise<void> => {
   ...
   await ipcRenderer.invoke(
     'local-file-save-from-arraybuffer',
     arrayBuffer,
     filePath,
+    basePath
   );
 };
```

`local-file-save-from-arraybuffer` has exactly ONE caller in the whole
repository, and this is it — which is good news for review completeness. It is
the channel that writes project resources (images, sounds, fonts) to their real
location on disk, and its base is the project's own folder: a resource named
by the project can only land inside the project.


HOW TO CHECK COMPLETENESS
-------------------------
Search the renderer for the four spellings that reach the privileged side:

```
downloadUrlsToLocalFiles(          -> 9 call sites (8 exporters + cloud handler)
local-file-download                 -> 1 send site (inside LocalFileDownloader.js)
local-file-save-from-arraybuffer    -> 1 send site (inside LocalFileResourceMover.js)
downloadBlobToLocalFile(            -> 1 call site (inside the same mover)
```

Each send must pass a base. In our tree all of them do, and the full-suite
type check (`npm run flow`) plus the lint pass are what prove it — an omitted
base is a runtime refusal, not a compile error, so the code review is the real
check here, not the compiler.

A note on something that LOOKS related but is not: `archiveFiles` in
`newIDE/app/src/Utils/BrowserArchiver.js` also takes a `basePath` argument, and
`ObjectExporterDialog.js` passes `''` to it. That is the browser-side zip
archiver and it never touches these IPC channels, so it is outside this
change. Do not "fix" it as part of this patch.


BEHAVIOUR CHANGE FOR CALLERS
----------------------------
A call whose `filePath` falls outside its declared `basePath` now fails with
`Refused: the download target is outside the base folder.` instead of
succeeding. In our tree no legitimate call site hit that — verified by running
the online-export and cloud-resource paths end to end, and worth re-verifying
the same way on any branch this is applied to. That end-to-end check is the one
manual step in this patch that the automated suite cannot do for you.
